cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Solar Flare Prediction & Space Weather Alert System Using Machine Learning
message: >-
  If you refer to this project, please cite it as below. If you use the
  underlying data, cite the SWAN-SF dataset separately (see references).
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Madhavan
    family-names: G
    affiliation: >-
      Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Chennai Institute of
      Technology (Autonomous), Chennai, India
  - given-names: Sriram
    family-names: Sivakumar
    affiliation: >-
      Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Chennai Institute of
      Technology (Autonomous), Chennai, India
abstract: >-
  A leakage-safe machine learning pipeline that forecasts whether a solar active
  region will produce a major (GOES M- or X-class) flare in the 24 hours
  following a 12-hour observation window, using the 24 SHARP magnetic-field
  parameters of the SWAN-SF benchmark. Seven candidates (logistic regression and
  random forest, point-in-time and temporal feature sets) are compared under a
  chronological partition split in which no active region is shared between
  train, validation and test. The model and its decision threshold are fixed on
  the validation partition before the test partition is scored once. The
  selected model, L2-regularised logistic regression on 144 temporal features
  with C=0.01, reaches a true skill statistic of 0.853 and a precision-recall
  AUC of 0.489 on the locked test partition, at 91.6 percent recall and 16.2
  percent precision. Delivered as an academic prototype with a static offline
  dashboard, not as an operational space-weather warning service.
keywords:
  - solar flare prediction
  - space weather
  - SWAN-SF
  - SHARP parameters
  - multivariate time series
  - class imbalance
  - true skill statistic
  - data leakage
  - logistic regression
  - random forest
